
# Example firmware setup for the LilygoWatch smartwatch

There's an [example script here](setup-dudley.sh) for firmware setup,
based on [Bill Dudley's repository](https://github.com/wfdudley/T-watch-2020)
(thanks Bill!).

Use it with arduino-esp32 core version 1.0.6, (with ESP IDF 3.3.5; it won't
currently compile with IDF 4+).

It should work reliably on Ubuntu 20.04, and is likely to work on MacOS or on
other Debian derivatives. On Windoze you might get lucky if you have Cygwin or
WSL or other GNU Linux emulation.

To run, do

```bash
  ./setup-dudley.sh
```

Then (if using the Arduino IDE) copy the libraries to your Arduino/ directory,
e.g.:

```bash
  mkdir -p ~/Arduino/libraries
  rsync -av lib/ ~/Arduino/Libraries/
```

If you need to install the Arduino ESP32 core, then make sure you've not got
old Arduino config left over in `~/.arduino15`, then fire up the Arduino IDE.
(Version 1.8.13 or thereabouts will work.)

Open `File>Preferences` and add the stable release boards manager URL:

```bash
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/espressif/arduino-esp32/gh-pages/package_esp32_index.json
```

Select `Tools>Board>Boards Manager`, install esp32 1.0.6 and then select TTGO
T-Watch from `Tools>Boards`. Select the port. Off you go!

(To set wifi and timezone without using your fingers, edit
`DudleyWatch/my_WiFi.h` and `personal_info.h`.)


## Troubleshooting

If you've previously installed a version of the arduino-esp32 core > 1.0.6
(i.e. 2.0.0-alpha at time of writing) you will likely get compile errors.
Solution: reinstall your SDK, e.g. by deleting `~/.arduino15` and the
`Arduino` directory, and starting again. Note that the `magic.sh` installs
usually include a version of the core, so be careful if using one of those.

Other IDEs and SDKs: chaos! Confusion! See chapter 2 and/or post on the
discussion board.
